insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('422701ab-d591-5142-96ff-ff2840b1a888', 'rg5-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Sea Otters and the Kelp Forest$q$, $q$Along some rocky coasts, a tall seaweed called kelp grows in thick underwater forests. Kelp can grow taller than a four-story building. Fish hide among its long blades, and crabs and snails live near its base.

Kelp has an enemy: the sea urchin. A sea urchin is a small, spiny animal that crawls along the ocean floor. Urchins chew through the bottom of the kelp, where it holds on to the rocks. When that part is eaten, the whole plant breaks loose and drifts away.

This is where sea otters come in. Sea otters eat many sea urchins. An otter dives down, grabs an urchin, and floats on its back to eat it. Because otters keep the number of urchins low, the kelp forest can keep growing.

In places where otters disappeared, scientists noticed a big change. Urchins spread across the sea floor with nothing to stop them. They ate kelp until large areas were bare rock. Fish that needed the kelp for shelter moved away.

When otters returned to some of these coasts, the pattern reversed. Urchin numbers dropped, and the kelp slowly grew back. The fish returned too. A small furry animal turned out to protect an entire forest under the sea.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
