-- Passage rg5-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('af1b117f-1ab6-5725-a077-3052e9869a0d', 'rg5-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Cutting Ice from Frozen Lakes$q$, $q$Long before homes had refrigerators, people still found ways to keep food cold. In many northern towns, they used ice cut from frozen lakes.

Ice harvesting happened in the coldest weeks of winter. Workers waited until the lake ice was very thick. Then they scraped away the snow, marked the ice into a grid, and sawed it into large blocks. Horses pulled the heavy blocks to shore.

The blocks were stored in icehouses. These buildings had thick walls, and workers packed sawdust between the blocks. The sawdust kept warm air away from the ice. Because of this packing, ice cut in January could last until late summer.

In warm months, an iceman delivered blocks to homes by wagon. Families kept the ice in a wooden icebox with a metal lining. Cold air from the ice kept milk, butter, and meat fresh for days.

Ice harvesting depended on the weather. A warm winter meant thin ice, and thin ice meant there was not enough to sell. In those years, the price of ice went up.

Later, people invented machines that could make ice in factories. Electric refrigerators followed and became common in homes. Families no longer needed the iceman, and most icehouses closed. Lakes that once rang with the sound of saws became quiet places for skating again.$q$, 'draft')
