   $q$Which chain of events best explains how the new signals help Riverton's cars burn less fuel?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Lights use timers, so cars wait longer, so drivers turn off their engines to save fuel.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Sensors measure fuel, so the computer warns drivers, so they buy cars that use less.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Cars burn less fuel, so they drive faster, so they spend less time at red lights.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-relationship"},{"text":"Lights respond to traffic, so cars stop less, so engines run while sitting still less often.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Lights respond to traffic, so cars stop less, so engines run while sitting still less often.$q$, $q$Paragraphs 3 and 5 connect the steps: signals respond to traffic, drivers spend less time stopped, and cars burn less fuel because they sit still with engines running less often. The choice "Cars burn less fuel, so they drive faster" reverses cause and effect.$q$, 'draft', 'dc9c3009-8bec-5c9a-a029-624619b4617c')
